{
  "id": "ukrainian_canadian_zabava",
  "name": "Zabava (Ukrainian-Canadian prairie dance-band music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Dance-band music of the Ukrainian communities of the Canadian prairies, rooted in the block settlements of immigrants from Galicia and Bukovyna who arrived from the end of the nineteenth century, and played at weddings, community-hall dances (zabavy) and festivals. Bands mix kolomyika, polka, waltz and hutsulka dance tunes and Ukrainian songs with country and pop, using violin, accordion and tsymbaly (hammered dulcimer) over electric bass, guitar and drum kit, and Winnipeg's V Records was among the labels that issued it on LP. The hammered-dulcimer card stands in for the tsymbaly.",
  "recipe": "classic North American mid-1940s zabava, drinking Song, European branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. fiddle, piano accordion, hammered dulcimer, single coil electric guitar, electric bass, machine hammered symmetric pattern organized rows full lathed both sides lathed drum kit. wooden barn. passive ribbon, tube pre, German tube console. Studer 30ips tape.",
